From: Michiel <367462+meghuizen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 12:16:59 +0200
Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/3] fs: less work per open()

An open() of an existing file takes a dentry reference in
complete_walk(), takes a second one in do_dentry_open() and drops the
first in terminate_walk(). Patch 2 hands the walk's reference to the
file, so an open/close cycle does two lockref operations on the dentry
instead of four; this matters when many processes open the same file.
Patch 3 allocates the struct file only once an open needs it, so opens
that fail in the walk (ENOENT and friends) no longer allocate and free
one. Patch 1 is an independent selftests Makefile fix that makes the
openat2 tests build again; it can be taken separately.

RFC because patch 2 overlaps with work Mateusz Guzik has posted (see
below the --- line of patch 2).

Measured in an x86-64 guest (nested KVM, 16 vCPUs), each patch alone on
the base commit, 2 boots per patch interleaved with 8 base boots, perf
stat in the guest:

  #  impact                          result
  1  low: test coverage              openat2 tests build and pass
  2  medium: many processes opening  16 processes, one ext4 file:
     one file                        -38% kernel cycles per open
  3  medium: opens that fail in the  ENOENT: -22% (ext4), -13% (tmpfs)
     walk (ENOENT)                   kernel instructions per open

Neither patch 2 nor patch 3 changes the kernel instruction count of a
successful single-process open beyond the per-boot spread (about 4%).

Testing:
- Each commit: the touched objects compile with W=1 (x86-64 config with
  SELinux, BPF LSM, ext4; gcc 15.2), no warnings; patches 2 and 3 also
  compile applied alone on the base commit. The three patches apply in
  order on the base commit.
- checkpatch --strict: clean apart from the missing Signed-off-by.
- Runtime, per patch and all together, 2 boots each: the openat2
  selftests (openat2_test, resolve_test, rename_attack_test,
  emptypath_test); an error-path test (O_CREAT|O_TRUNC, O_DIRECT failing
  after the open, EACCES from may_open(), ENOTDIR) followed by umount of
  the ext4 and tmpfs mounts to catch a leaked reference; no kernel
  warnings. The instruction and cycle counts in patches 2 and 3.
- Patch 1: the four openat2 programs also build and run on a 6.18 host.

Not tested:
- Hardware other than the nested KVM guest; wall-clock throughput.
- The file-max limit (patch 3), network filesystems and ->atomic_open()
  users, filesystems other than ext4 and tmpfs.
- Any architecture other than x86-64.
- sparse/smatch (not available on the build host).
- Full kernel build other than the tested x86-64 config; allmodconfig.

Tool assistance: the changes, changelogs and measurements in this series
were produced with Claude (Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5) as a coding
assistant, prompted to find and fix performance problems in the VFS and
to measure them. Each patch carries an Assisted-by tag.

